r.  JJ is a decent pair.  AA is the top pair (since I\'ve kept track of the cards, and there are no 2s left).  Then there\'s the King, and the 2, which is nearly unbeatable, especially since all the other 2\'s have been played.\n\nThe alternative is to play the 7-card straight: 3-4-5-6-7-8-9, and I\'m left with 9, JJ, AA, K, 2. But, again, that is breaking up the pair of 9s. Single 9 is a risky play since the Landlord might have something to beat it, and I\'d be stuck with a single 9 which is awkward.\n\nSo I think Option A, playing the 6-card straight is the better play here.\n\n**Risk Assessment and Strategic Decision: Straight is the Play**\n\nThe Landlord could have a similar straight or a bomb, but with 14 cards remaining, that\'s less likely than I might assume. I can\'t just pass the play to the Landlord; I need to keep the momentum. So I\'m deciding: playing the 6-card straight is the play! It cleans up my hand a lot, and gives me better chances with my remaining cards.\n\n**Final Hand Analysis and Justification**\n\nOnce I get rid of the 3-4-5-6-7-8, I\'m left with 99, JJ, AA, K, and 2.  My pair of Aces is the highest pair in the game (given that all 2s are accounted for). The 2 will be a powerful play to control the game. This sets up well to support the rest of the game and hopefully get me to win. Playing this set gets me out of the game\'s "trash" cards and moves me closer to a winning hand.\n\n**The Move**\n\nThe optimal move is to play the straight ♥3, ♦4, ♦5, ♦6, ♦7, ♦8.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nEvaluation: I possess a contiguous sequence of low cards (3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8) that forms a straight. Playing this combination is critical to clearing out the "junk" portion of my hand. If I were to play these as singles, I would lose tempo. I also have the option to include the 9 in the straight, but doing so would break my pair of 9s, leaving me with a single 9 which is difficult to play. By playing the 6-card straight, I retain the pair of 9s, which sits well wit